# Box64 emulator settings, copied to /data/config/box64.rc on first start.
# Everything is commented out: with nothing uncommented, Box64's own defaults
# apply, and those are the fastest ones.
#
# A variable already set in the container environment (docker run -e, or the
# compose "environment:" block) WINS over this file. The server log prints, at
# every start, which value came from where.
#
# After editing: docker compose restart valheim
#
# Full documentation: https://github.com/ptitSeb/box64/blob/main/docs/USAGE.md


### Compatibility ladder
#
# A heavily multithreaded program such as a Unity server can be sensitive to the
# memory model, especially on an ARMv8.0-A CPU without the LSE atomic
# instructions (the Cortex-A72 of a Raspberry Pi 4, for instance). If you hit
# instability -- crashes, a corrupted world, inconsistent behaviour -- climb the
# ladder BELOW IN ORDER, one rung at a time, testing after each: every rung
# costs performance.
#
#   1. BOX64_DYNAREC_STRONGMEM=1      then 2, then 3
#   2. BOX64_DYNAREC_BIGBLOCK=1       then 0
#   3. BOX64_DYNAREC_CALLRET=0
#   4. BOX64_DYNAREC_SAFEFLAGS=2


### Memory model

# Emulate the strong x86 memory model. 0 = fast, 3 = safest.
# Values: 0 1 2 3 4 -- default: 0
#BOX64_DYNAREC_STRONGMEM=1

# Only emit aligned atomics. Values: 0 1 -- default: 0
#BOX64_DYNAREC_ALIGNED_ATOMICS=1


### Code generation

# Size of the blocks compiled by the JIT. Bigger = faster, less forgiving.
# Values: 0 1 2 3 -- default: 2
#BOX64_DYNAREC_BIGBLOCK=1

# CALL/RET opcode optimisation. Values: 0 1 2 3 -- default: 2
#BOX64_DYNAREC_CALLRET=0

# Flag emulation on CALL/RET and edge cases. Values: 0 1 2 -- default: 1
#BOX64_DYNAREC_SAFEFLAGS=2

# Wait for a block to be compiled (0 = interpret it in the meantime).
# Values: 0 1 -- default: 1
#BOX64_DYNAREC_WAIT=1


### Floating point

# Fast NaN handling. Values: 0 1 -- default: 1
#BOX64_DYNAREC_FASTNAN=0

# Fast rounding. Values: 0 1 2 -- default: 1
#BOX64_DYNAREC_FASTROUND=0


### Misc

# Expose AVX to the emulated program. Values: 0 1 2 -- default: 2 on ARM64
#BOX64_AVX=1

# Intercept malloc operators. Values: 0 1 2 -- default: 0
#BOX64_MALLOC_HACK=1

# Box64 verbosity in the log. Values: 0 1 2 3 -- default: 0
# Useful to diagnose a crash, very verbose.
#BOX64_LOG=1

# Hide the Box64 banner at start-up. Values: 0 1 -- default: 0
#BOX64_NOBANNER=1
